#{[17592186097810 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with decimal coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.2x+y=1 \\\\ 0.15x-0.04y=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the system easier to solve, we turn it into a system involving integer coefficients only.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by $10,$ or $100,$ or $1000,$ etc. depending on how many decimal places we need to eliminate. In this particular instance:\n\n- We need to eliminate one decimal place in the top equation, so we multiply by $10.$\n\n- We need to eliminate two decimal places in the bottom equation, so we multiply by $100.$\n\nSo, we multiply the first equation by $10$ and the second equation by $100,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{red}0.2} x + y = 1 & {\\color{blue}| \\times  10} \\\\ {\\color{red}0.15} x - {\\color{red}0.04} y = {\\color{red}0.2} & {\\color{blue}| \\times  100}\\end{array} \\right. }\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x + 10 y = 10 \\\\ 15 x - 4 y = 20\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can solve the system of equations using either substitution or elimination."] [17592186097919 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with fractional coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y=-4 \\\\ 7x-5y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can turn the system into an equivalent system involving only integer coefficients. This will make it easier to solve.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by its lowest common denominator. In this instance, multiplying the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions $(4)$ will do the job:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {\\color{red}2}}} x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{ {\\color{red}4}}} y = - 4 & {\\color{blue}| \\hspace{0.222em} \\times  4} \\\\ 7 x - 5 y = - 2 & \\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x + 5 y = - 16 \\\\ 7 x - 5 y = - 2\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the system using, for example, the elimination method. Notice that the $5y$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-5y$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}2x+\\cancel{5y} & =-16 \\\\ 7x-\\cancel{5y} & =-2 \\\\ \\hline 9x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=-2$ into the equation $7x - 5y= -2$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-5y & =-2 \\\\ 7(-2)-5y & =-2 \\\\ -14-5y & =-2 \\\\ -5y & =12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2, \\,y=-\\dfrac{12}{5}.$"] [17592186101932 "Suppose we want to find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)\n$$ for the piecewise function given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an idea of the behavior of the function around $x=0,$ we can plot the function.\n\n![](images/78/78a832c65a4bb5862694717f52704a61660bd81a34eb7a72cfeeb98a52bb50f1.png)\n\nTo compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x),\n$$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3^{x+1}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{3}^{x+1} \\\\  & ={3}^{0+1} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt {x^2+9}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe left and right-sided limits both exist and are equal. So, the overall limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x) =3.\n$$"] [17592186107116 "It is often more convenient to use polar coordinates to determine the points of intersection of two curves. This is especially true if we are dealing with two circles. \n\nSuppose, for example, that we are given the circles ${\\color{}r=\\sqrt2\\,  {\\sin{\\theta}}}\\,$ and $\\, {\\color{}r=1},$ as shown in the figure below. How do we determine  the polar coordinates of the points of intersection of these two curves?  \n\n![](images/82/8225490259dfcf03d505b7f68a486a8246a46b29f6047f1c066d0e9436f8b55f.png)\n\n \n\nTo determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we must solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}, \\qquad \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $r=1$ at both intersection points. Therefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are \n\n$$\n(r,\\theta)=\\bigg(1, \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\bigg)\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad(r,\\theta)=\\bigg(1, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\bigg).\n$$"] [17592186125892 "Suppose we want to find the **global maximum** and **global minimum** of a function $f(x,y).$ Recall that the global maximum and minimum of a function are collectively known as **global extrema.** \n\nThe procedure we will describe for finding the global extrema is based on a general principle called the **extreme-value theorem,** which states the following:\n\n*Every function that is continuous on a closed, bounded region $D$ has global extrema (i.e., both a global maximum and a global minimum) on $D$*.\n\nLet's consider the function $f(x,y) =  e^{x^2- y^2}$ on the closed  region $D=\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2: \\,  x^2 +y^2  \\leq 1\\}.$ How do we find the global extrema?\n\nTo find the global extrema of a differentiable function on a closed, bounded region $D,$ we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1**:  Determine the critical points of $f$ in the interior of $D.$\n\n![](images/50/5057a4a96daa4819a70e733519c89802c28bde184183db2b4efbe69d3db36e56.png)\n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve $\\boldsymbol f' = \\mathbf 0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n𝒇'(x,y) = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 x {e}^{ {x}^{2} - {y}^{2}} & - 2 y {e}^{ {x}^{2} - {y}^{2}}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $x^2 + y^2 =1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle \\cos{t}, \\sin{t} \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (0,2\\pi),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)})^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has three solutions, namely \n\n$$\nt =\\dfrac\\pi 2, \\: \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}2 \\in (0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval, namely, $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\:  \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ for each value of $t$, we obtain the following candidate points:\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\: (0,1), \\: (-1,0), \\: (0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nEvaluating $f$ at the five points we've found gives the following table of values:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(x,y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-1,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0,-1)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f(x,y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$e$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1/e$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$e$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1/e$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $\\dfrac1e$ at the points $(0,1), \\, (0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $e$ at the points $(1,0), \\, (-1,0).$"] [17592186135049 "We've already seen how to find the orthogonal projection of a vector onto a subspace in the case when the subspace is defined using an orthogonal basis. In this lesson, we'll learn how to do the same thing in the case of non-orthogonal bases.\n\nFor example, consider the following vectors:\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \\:\n\t\\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{x}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nHow can we find the orthogonal projection $\\mathbf{x}_S$ of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}?$ Notice that since $\\mathbf a_1\\cdot \\mathbf a_2 \\neq 0,$ the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are not orthogonal.\n\nAs usual, we would like to write $\\mathbf{x}$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\in S$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\!\\in S^\\perp.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a5576fcaf9e0be6fc14978eb91dcbaceee75cbfddaa537fdb05d34b8e31e2e0.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp,$ that is, $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\perp S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expression for $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ into the system to get\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( \\mathbf{x} - {k}_{1} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} - {k}_{2} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} ) \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} = 0 \\\\ ( \\mathbf{x} - {k}_{1} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} - {k}_{2} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} ) \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{k}_{1} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} )} + {k}_{2} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} )} = {\\color{red}( \\mathbf{x} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} )} \\\\ {k}_{1} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} )} + {k}_{2} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} )} = {\\color{red}( \\mathbf{x} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} )} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe can write this system in an equivalent matrix form as follows:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} - \\\\ - \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} -\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{k}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t=\n{\\color{red}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} - \\\\ - \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} -\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}| \\\\ \\mathbf{x} \\\\ |\\end{array} \\right]}\n}\n$$\n\nIf we denote \n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nand $\\mathbf{k}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{k}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\nA^TA\\mathbf{k} = A^T\\mathbf{x}\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{k}=(A^TA)^{-1}A^T\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nAlso, we note that  $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2$ can be expressed as $\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x}\t= A\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =Ak \\\\  & =A({A}^{T}A)^{-1}{A}^{T}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a formula for the orthogonal projection!\n\nNow, we return to our example. Using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 11\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note**: Pay attention to the order in which we carried out the multiplication. Carrying out the operations in the order shown above can save us a lot of work!"] [17592186135062 "Consider the following data set:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$100$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$110$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$120$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$130$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1}=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 110 \\\\ 1 & 120 \\\\ 1 & 130\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 15\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$"] [17592186135063 "We've seen how to construct a simple linear regression model given some bivariate data $(x,y).$ We can generalize this technique to fit data to an arbitrary polynomial. We call this process **polynomial regression**.\n\nThe following table gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Goods $(x)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Profit $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSuppose we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/93/93fe1784b89ce4c892c84a03a0dc80909f16f46ac463c5cc30b40f869a7802bf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+1{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=16 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}16 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the curve that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}22.5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the quadratic curve of best fit (regression curve) is \n\n$$\ny = 22.5 - 9x + 2x^2.\n$$"] [17592186135064 "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 1 $(x_1)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 2 $(x_2)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Profit $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\n  \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2}=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2}=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**."] [17592186135098 "Let's consider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown below:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large A}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large Q}\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large R}\n$$\n\nSuppose also that we are given the following system of equations that connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{2}+3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHow do we find $R?$\nFirst, let's rewrite the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\mathbf{q}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\mathbf{q}}_{1} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} + 3 {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} - 2 {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} + {\\mathbf{q}}_{2}\\end{array} \\right. }\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1} = {\\color{red}2} {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} = {\\color{red}- 9} {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} + {\\color{blue}3} {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} = {\\color{red}8} {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} - {\\color{blue}4} {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} + {\\color{purple}4} {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIn this new system, the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_1$ correspond to the first-row entries of $R,$ the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_2$ correspond to the second-row entries of $R,$ and the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_3$ correspond to the third-row entries of $R.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large A}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large Q}\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}- 9} & {\\color{red}8} \\\\ 0 & {\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}- 4} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\color{purple}4}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large R}.\n$$\n\nTo see why this is, we can take the transpose of both sides of $A=QR{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =QR \\\\ {A}^{T} & =(QR)^{T} \\\\ {A}^{T} & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T} \\\\ \\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{bmatrix}}^{{A}^{T}} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{{R}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we were to expand this matrix product, we would obtain our system of equations."] [17592186135143 "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and\n$$\nQ = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\tR = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & - 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$\nR \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4(2) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ is ${\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$"] [17592186163339 "Suppose the continuous random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_X(x) =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{e}^{- x} , \\hspace{1em} & x > 0 \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nLet $Y= 1+2X.$ Let's use the distribution function method to find the probability density function of $Y.$\n\nFirst, note that $Y = 1+2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $x > 0,$ we have $y > 1.$\n\n![](images/54/544e551755f4c8a575ef227b77fe520deec203f08654eec31109e496141aa91c.png)\n\nLet's consider $y > 1.$ We write down the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ and then use our transformation to isolate $X$ in the parentheses. This will allow us to write $F_Y$ in terms of $F_X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X+1\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y-1) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $y > 1,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({F}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right)\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{-(y-1)/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}, & \\,y>1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy (and often worthwhile) to check that this is a valid probability density function."] [17592186167990 "Consider an **inhomogeneous** linear system of ordinary differential equations in component form:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={a}_{11}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{12}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={a}_{21}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{22}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we can package the unknown functions into vectors\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} ( t ) \\\\ {x}_{2} ( t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThen, the system can be written as\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),\n\\qquad\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{g}(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{f}_{1} ( t ) \\\\ {f}_{2} ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nOften, the **forcing functions** $f_1(t), f_2(t)$ are linear combinations of a few basic functions (such as $e^{3t},$ $t^2,$ or $\\sin t$).\nIn that case, it is useful to write $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ as a sum of those basic functions times constant vectors.\n\nFor example, let's write down the system of linear ODEs below in matrix form.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+8{e}^{3t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+10{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 8+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 0+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}4 & 2 \\\\ - 3 & 5\\end{array} \\right]}}_{A} \\mathbf{x}(t) + \n\\underbrace{e^{3t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}8 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]} + \ne^{4t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 10\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{g}(t)}\n$$\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}(t) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} ( t ) \\\\ {x}_{2} ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}.$"] [17592186168000 "Sketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ngiven that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=-4$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},$ respectively.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]} = \nc_1  e^{-4t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} + \nc_2 e^{2t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny =-\\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_1 e^{-4t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/14/1459d79913dec89577405a506b8d4adbbf4cb05abb51948eedae76d8f71544b8.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny= x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_2 e^{2t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/fd/fd53399b3d0c5ec40adcf91aaae4aca5ce55a9a7b574e9223fde615afc3b769f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/bf/bf0c26af74326dd108d42eec1493b0ddf64cfdf0744c91b55e732b80b7d0835c.png)"] [17592186168006 "Consider the following system of linear differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system.\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n$$\n\\lambda = 1, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{v} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{w} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_1 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move toward infinity in the phase plane as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\nc_2 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}t \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{t} \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.98$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.61$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.32$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4fbc1650377d580d7129701d745dc8cad89d2d1d061b8f6dca8127f0ce062dc8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/49/499391b5bc015bd96bbe85d72e3937e87a5e89081951aaad0a752582cb87f15f.png)"] [17592186168007 "Let's sketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ngiven that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},$ respectively.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]} = \nc_1 e^{5t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]} + \nc_2 e^{2t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny= 2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_1 e^{5t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{5t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c7/c7c824e97d40d2f982525040d5f0ba697c34c40ff51700ec3952d2909d566e21.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_2 e^{2t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c3/c3030613b03a1afabd964d9494bf2359bd6ce5f021c731feda9607a81aaf5df6.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n0 < \\lambda_2 < \\lambda_1\n$$\n\nthus, $\\lambda_1 = 5$ is the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=5,$ solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/43/43c6294fa7f68b21422eee71d35edd5f7880b3b3c9e23a2a8078090416cded05.png)"] [17592186173153 "In general, the cross-term of a quadratic equation can be eliminated by an orthogonal change of variables. That is, one of the kind \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n  where the columns of $P$ form an orthonormal  eigenbasis for the corresponding quadratic form's matrix.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C$ be a plane curve defined as \n\n$$\n\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1x_2 -2\\sqrt2x_1+2\\sqrt2x_2 =8.\n$$\n\nHow do we determine the type of our curve?\n\nNotice that the quadratic form $2x_1x_2$ can be reduced to canonical form $y_1^2-y_2^2$ using the change of variables\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]} =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}}\\end{array} \\right]} \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]} = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}}\\end{array} \\right]} \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} {y}_{1} - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} {y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} {y}_{1} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} {y}_{2} ,\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe know that this change of variables reduces the corresponding quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = 2x_1x_2$ to its canonical form. Indeed, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=8\\,⟹\\,{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+4{y}_{2}=8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n and, by completing the  square for the variable $y_2$,  we get the equation \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the resulting equation in the $y_1,y_2$ variables is the following.\n\n![](images/d5/d532f5207a9d518f92024c86689285834b86c961d0f647d6ee99ad6dd9c7c329.png)\n\nNotice that we can write down the change-of-variables matrix as\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}}\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\cos   {45}^{\\circ } & - \\sin   {45}^{\\circ } \\\\ \\sin   {45}^{\\circ } & \\cos   {45}^{\\circ }\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nwhich is a rotation matrix. Looking at the diagram above, we see that going from one coordinate system to another can be viewed as a rotation of our axes by $45^\\circ.$\n\n**Watch out!** In general, this action is an orthogonal transformation (it can be a rotation, a reflection, or a combination of such transformations).\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis (standard) equation represents a hyperbola in which  both semiaxes are equal to $2.$      \n\nIn the diagram below we represent the curve in the $z_1,z_2$ variables.\n\n![](images/c3/c36709f752bd9c319662e7e2150c54031cc8fb3934be37711c65e7a7668829ca.png)\n\nLooking at the diagram above, we see that in the final step we simply translated our system by $(0,2)$ in $y$-coordinates.\n\n**Note:** Finding the simplest equation of a quadratic curve can be viewed as the following series of steps:\n\n- **Step 1**. We eliminate the cross-term by reducing the quadratic part to canonical form. Geometrically, this is equivalent to an orthogonal transformation of our coordinate system.\n\n- **Step 2**. We eliminate the linear terms by completing the squares. Geometrically, this is equivalent to a translation of our coordinate system."] [17592186173155 "A quadratic equation with no cross-terms can be rewritten in standard form by completing the square. Let's see how this is done in the following example. \n\nSuppose we are given the equation  \n\n$$\n2x_1^2+x_2^2 - 4x_1 + 4x_2 = 2,\n$$\n\nwhat type of curve does it represent?\n\nFirst, we complete the squares for the variables $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}+4-4) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-2+({x}_{2}+2)^{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}+({x}_{2}+2)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-1, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{8}.$"] [17592186199484 "Suppose we want to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2 \\leq 1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5ac34145b3911b19eb3775a915e8f0462c78502be6d6292e7682265c86eafce7.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find the extrema, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the critical points of $f$ inside the domain $x^2+y^2 < 1.$\n\n- Then, we find the extreme values of $f$ subject to the constraint $x^2+y^2=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\n- Finally, we determine the maximum and minimum values by evaluating $f$ at the candidate points.\n\nRecall that the critical points of a function $f(x,y)$ are the solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ Now, since the derivative of $f$ is the transpose of its gradient $\\nabla f,$ the critical points are also given by the solutions to $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0.$\n\nSo, we can find the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ Note that this point belongs to our domain.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + y^2  = 1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1), \\qquad (0,0).\n$$\n\nNote that we add the critical point $(0,0)$ to the list of the candidates above.\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points: \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$(x,y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(\\pm 1,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0, \\pm 1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0, 0)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x,y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $0$ at the point $(0, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at the points $(0, \\pm 1).$"] [17592186199500 "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = -y = -z+1.\n$$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\n \n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx = -y = -z+1 = t  \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = t \\\\ y = - t \\\\ z = - t + 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $$\n\\displaystyle (\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2})\n$$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example."] [17592186218320 "Consider the line $y = x + 2$ and the parabola $y = x^2 + x + 1,$ shown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/9d/9d7ca10163c9b5096f90e19bdb50d98881bd55e92cbaea4aa5af6696e728f9f3.png)\n\nWe can see from the diagram that there are two points of intersection. So let's calculate the coordinates of these intersection points.\n\nAn intersection point is a common solution of the equations $y = x + 2$ and $y = x^2 + x + 1.$ In other words, to get the coordinates of the intersection points, we have to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+x+1 \\\\ y=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and then solving for $x$ using our knowledge of quadratic equations, we get\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {x}^{2} + x + 1 & \\displaystyle  = x + 2 \\\\ \\displaystyle {x}^{2} + \\cancel{x} + 1 & \\displaystyle  = \\cancel{x} + 2 \\\\ \\displaystyle {x}^{2} + 1 & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\\\ \\displaystyle {x}^{2} - 1 & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle ( x + 1 ) ( x - 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle x & \\displaystyle  = - 1 , 1.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting $x=−1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =(-1)+2 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,1).$\n\n- Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =(1)+2 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(1,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the points of intersection are $\\left(-1,\\,1 \\right)$ and $\\left(1,\\,3 \\right).$"] [17592186222488 "Suppose a $2 \\times 2$ system has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2\\neq 0$ with eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nThen, the eigenvector direction for $\\lambda_1=0$ gives a **line of equilibria**:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_E = c_1 \\mathbf{v}_1, \\qquad c_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nThe other eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$ determines what happens off the equilibrium line:\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ trajectories move *away* from the equilibrium line (*unstable* direction).\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ trajectories move *toward* the equilibrium line (*stable* direction).\n\nA typical solution has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + c_2 e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2,\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSo trajectories are *parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2,$* shifted by the equilibrium vector $c_1\\mathbf{v}_1.$\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we know the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=3,$ with corresponding eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},$ respectively. \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system. \n\nThe general solution of the system is\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]} = \nc_1 {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} + \nc_2 e^{3t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\n- First, notice that $\\mathbf{x}_E(t) = c_1 {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/41/4186349824bab0f592f8e951f2b0ae7472e7879c693b0271af9e824bbc67f112.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ has the equation \n$y= -x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_2 e^{3t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on that line. Since $e^{3t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n![](images/95/95c8829e56ce60e3fd877757c0063878e283f65a2da327bbd64ae80d725fbd81.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7bb6a132d08721ef0d96e6e47dc3089de551e9551524f47de0af495a0b9418f5.png)"]}
